# SA-02m: Armbian RAM logging is OFF — this file is repo-owned
# (etc/default/armbian-ramlog, installed by scripts/01-system.sh: install.sh,
# refresh, offline full update, and the golden image built from such a donor).
# NOT in the OTA deploy map, on purpose: OTA restarts nothing, so flipping the
# hooks off there would also stop the nightly flush while the cron-relinquished
# journal stays in /run until reboot — worse than before. Installed over
# Armbian's own copy; an armbian-bsp upgrade may flag it to dpkg as a modified
# conffile — keep this version.
#
# Why off. On SA-02m the RAM log was never really on: the tmpfs is not mounted
# (armbian-ramlog.service disabled, /var/log on the eMMC root). Only the hooks
# ran, against a disk /var/log, and each one did harm (bench 1.135, 2026-09-23):
#   1. The cron armbian-truncate-logs (every 15 min) ran
#      `journalctl --relinquish-var`: the journal lived in /run (RAM) except
#      at the nightly flush, so a power cut lost the day's journal.
#   2. logrotate.service ExecStartPre=armbian-ramlog write rsynced /var/log/
#      into /var/log.hdd/ on the SAME eMMC every midnight (82 MB on 23 Sep;
#      /var/log.hdd held 478 MB of duplicates).
#   3. Its ExecStartPost=armbian-ramlog postrotate copied the pre-rotation
#      files back over the fresh logs: nginx/sa02m_access.log, rotated at
#      00:00 on 23 Sep, again held 20-22 Sep — rotation defeated for every log.
#   4. At >=75 % rootfs use the same cron truncates every *.log under /var/log
#      and deletes rotated files, every 15 min (1.135 was at 64 %).
# ENABLED=false makes the Armbian scripts exit before any of that.
#
# The journal policy itself (persistent storage, sync interval, size) has one
# home: etc/systemd/sa02m-journald.conf.
#
# The other keys are Armbian's, kept as on 1.135 so a re-enable is a one-word
# change.
ENABLED=false
SIZE=50M
USE_RSYNC=true
XTRA_RSYNC_FROM=()
